package Vixen.TextureGraph.Shaders

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// One sweep of the propagation doc 48 § 4.5's `Flood Fill` is, and § 4.5 is right that it is the
// highest-risk kernel in the catalogue.
//
// ## What a texel carries, and why the label *is* the bounding box
//
// `(minX, minY, maxX, maxY)` of the island as this texel has seen it so far, in texels. A background
// texel carries `-1` in every channel and is skipped by every merge.
//
// The obvious arrangement is a label chain that settles first and a bounds chain that follows it.
// This is one chain, because **the union of two adjacent in-texels' bounds is the merge either
// chain would have done**: adjacency *is* connectivity, so a merge needs no label to compare. The
// bounds are monotone, so the iteration has a fixed point and reaching it early is stable, and the
// settled minimum corner is a canonical name for the island that every one of its texels agrees on.
//
// ⚠ **So the island's identity is its bounding box, and two islands with the same bounding box are
// one island to every consumer of this node.** That is a real collision and it is not hypothetical —
// an L-shape and a small square tucked into its corner can share a box. It is the price of settling
// bounds and labels in one chain rather than two, it is what `TextureFloodOutput` documents, and the
// alternative costs twice the dispatches for a case a splatter never produces.
//
// ## ⚠ A sweep crosses a whole run, and why that is not a jump flood
//
// This kernel used to move bounds one texel per dispatch, so an island wider than the budget never
// settled, and it looked plausible doing it: Rikarin/Patina#98. A jump flood is the obvious cure and
// **it is wrong here**: a jump crosses background, and a texel that reads a neighbour across a gap
// merges two islands that do not touch.
//
// What can go far without crossing anything is a walk along one line of the image, stopping at the
// first background texel. So one invocation owns one row (or column, or diagonal), reads along it,
// and gives every texel of each run the union of that run. A run is connected by construction, so
// nothing is merged that does not touch, and an island of any width takes the same one dispatch.
// Sweeping rows and columns in turn, an island settles in about as many dispatches as a walk across
// it has corners: a rectangle in two, whatever its size, which is what stops the budget depending on
// the resolution.
//
// `diagonal` is therefore not a flag on the merge any more: an eight-connected flood is one whose
// chain also sweeps the two diagonals, which is `axis` 2 and 3. A four-connected one never asks for
// them, and that is the whole of the difference.
//
// **Each line is written by the one invocation that owns it**, so no two invocations touch a texel,
// and only the invocations on the edge the lines start from do any work. That is why the group size
// stays the evaluator's: every invocation still runs, and all but one per line return at once.
//
// ## ⚠ Round the wrap, a box is an arc
//
// Along an axis the plan wraps, the image is a circle, and an island over the seam is one island. A
// walk goes on past the edge, and `Math.Address` says which texel it has reached. Three things follow.
//
// **A line that closes has no first texel.** A row of a wrapping image comes back to where it began,
// so a run can go over any start. Such a line starts after its first background texel, and one with
// none is a single run the whole way round. On a torus a diagonal closes too, after the least common
// multiple of the sides, and only `Divisor` of them begin at the top edge. Along one axis it does not
// close, and it begins on the edge that does not wrap.
//
// **Two records of one island can only be merged where they meet.** A box is `min` to `max`, and with
// a wrap `max` can pass the image: `minX` stays in `0 .. width - 1` and a texel left of it is on the
// next lap. `Moved` puts a record in the frame of the walk that reached it, the walk takes its union,
// and `Settled` brings the result back. So every texel of an island still names the same record, and
// `TextureFloodOutput` still reads one name, size and UV off it.
//
// **An island round the whole axis has no ends**, so it is `0 .. width - 1`, which is the one box that
// says so: a span that reaches the side is that box whichever way the merges went. An arc only grows
// inside the island's shadow on the axis, so the fixed point is the same as it is on a plane.
//
// A clamped axis is the same code with nothing to do: a walk never leaves it, no texel is left of its
// box, and `Moved` moves nothing.
//
// ## ⚠ The number of sweeps is a property of the *shape*, and it is reported rather than guessed
//
// A while on the device is not an option — the fixed point is a property of the whole image and a
// workgroup can only wait for itself — so `TextureAnalysis.FloodFill` emits a **fixed budget** and
// the node appends `FloodResidual` and a `MinMaxReduce` chain after it. A host reads one texel and
// learns whether the budget was enough. **That is the difference between a truncated flood fill and a
// wrong picture nobody can account for**, and it is the whole reason this node is four kernels.
//
// ## 32 bits, always
//
// ⚠ A coordinate is stored, not an offset, so the record has to be exact on the integers across the
// image. A half stops at 2048 and quantises past it, which merges islands two texels apart, so the
// node keeps this record in `rgba32f` at every size and every bit depth, whatever the picture is in.

shader FloodBounds {
    /// The mask on the first dispatch, the previous sweep's bounds on every one after it.
    var source: Texture2D

    /// Non-zero when `source` is the mask rather than a record.
    var first: int = 1

    /// What counts as inside the mask, read off the red channel.
    var threshold: float = 0.5f

    /// Which lines this sweep walks: 0 rows · 1 columns · 2 and 3 the two diagonals.
    var axis: int = 0

    /// Non-zero when a run reaching the right edge goes on at the left. The plan's, as `seed` is: see
    /// `Math.Address`.
    var wrapX: int = 0

    /// The same for the bottom edge and the top.
    var wrapY: int = 0

    [Format("rgba16f")] var target: RWTexture2D<float4>

    /// A texel that is in no island.
    const val Background: float = -1f

    /// One texel's record, or its seeding. ⚠ The caller stays inside the image: a clamped tap would
    /// join two islands that are separated by exactly the image edge.
    func Record(x: int, y: int): float4 {
        val texel = source.Load(int3(x, y, 0))

        if (first == 0) {
            return texel
        }

        if (texel.x >= threshold) {
            return float4(float(x), float(y), float(x), float(y))
        }

        return float4(Background, Background, Background, Background)
    }

    /// The largest number both sides divide by, which is how many diagonals a torus has.
    func Divisor(width: int, height: int): int {
        var large = width
        var small = height

        while (small != 0) {
            val rest = large % small

            large = small
            small = rest
        }

        return large
    }

    /// Whether a line of this sweep begins at a texel. Every texel is on exactly one line.
    func Begins(x: int, y: int, width: int, height: int): bool {
        if (axis == 0) {
            return x == 0
        }

        if (axis == 1) {
            return y == 0
        }

        var near = 0

        if (axis == 3) {
            near = width - 1
        }

        if (wrapX != 0 && wrapY != 0) {
            return y == 0 && x < Divisor(width, height)
        }

        if (wrapX != 0) {
            return y == 0
        }

        if (wrapY != 0) {
            return x == near
        }

        return x == near || y == 0
    }

    /// Whether a line of this sweep comes back to where it began.
    func Closes(): bool {
        if (axis == 0) {
            return wrapX != 0
        }

        if (axis == 1) {
            return wrapY != 0
        }

        return wrapX != 0 && wrapY != 0
    }

    /// How many texels a line of this sweep has at most. One that leaves the image ends there.
    func Longest(width: int, height: int): int {
        if (axis == 0) {
            return width
        }

        if (axis == 1) {
            return height
        }

        if (wrapX != 0 && wrapY != 0) {
            return width / Divisor(width, height) * height
        }

        if (wrapX != 0) {
            return height
        }

        if (wrapY != 0) {
            return width
        }

        return max(width, height)
    }

    /// Whether a walk is still on the image, which along a wrapping axis it always is.
    func Inside(x: int, y: int, width: int, height: int): bool {
        return (wrapX != 0 || (x >= 0 && x < width)) && (wrapY != 0 || (y >= 0 && y < height))
    }

    /// Where a texel sits along an axis relative to a box that starts at `low`.
    func Lap(texel: int, low: float, extent: int): int {
        if (float(texel) < low) {
            return texel + extent
        }

        return texel
    }

    /// A record in the frame of the walk that reached it, at `x, y`, on the texel `texelX, texelY`.
    func Moved(record: float4, x: int, y: int, texelX: int, texelY: int, width: int, height: int): float4 {
        val byX = float(x - Lap(texelX, record.x, width))
        val byY = float(y - Lap(texelY, record.y, height))

        return record + float4(byX, byY, byX, byY)
    }

    /// One axis of a box in the image's frame, however far a walk went: `minimum, maximum`.
    func Arc(low: float, high: float, extent: int): float2 {
        val span = high - low + 1f
        val whole = float(extent)

        if (span >= whole) {
            return float2(0f, whole - 1f)
        }

        val start = low - (whole * floor(low / whole))

        return float2(start, start + span - 1f)
    }

    /// A walk's union as the record every texel of its run gets.
    func Settled(bounds: float4, width: int, height: int): float4 {
        val across = Arc(bounds.x, bounds.z, width)
        val down = Arc(bounds.y, bounds.w, height)

        return float4(across.x, down.x, across.y, down.y)
    }

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        val size = target.GetDimensions()
        val width = int(size.x)
        val height = int(size.y)
        val startX = int(id.x)
        val startY = int(id.y)

        if (startX >= width || startY >= height || !Begins(startX, startY, width, height)) {
            return
        }

        var stepX = 1
        var stepY = 1

        if (axis == 0) {
            stepY = 0
        }

        if (axis == 1) {
            stepX = 0
        }

        if (axis == 3) {
            stepX = -1
        }

        val longest = Longest(width, height)
        var skipped = 0

        if (Closes()) {
            var gap = 0

            while (gap < longest) {
                val tapX = Math.Address(startX + (gap * stepX), width, wrapX)
                val tapY = Math.Address(startY + (gap * stepY), height, wrapY)

                if (Record(tapX, tapY).x < 0f) {
                    skipped = gap

                    break
                }

                gap = gap + 1
            }
        }

        var x = startX + (skipped * stepX)
        var y = startY + (skipped * stepY)
        var left = longest

        while (left > 0 && Inside(x, y, width, height)) {
            val texelX = Math.Address(x, width, wrapX)
            val texelY = Math.Address(y, height, wrapY)
            val here = Record(texelX, texelY)
            var nextX = x + stepX
            var nextY = y + stepY
            var length = 1

            if (here.x < 0f) {
                target.Store(int2(texelX, texelY), float4(Background, Background, Background, Background))
            }

            if (here.x >= 0f) {
                val moved = Moved(here, x, y, texelX, texelY, width, height)
                var low = float2(moved.x, moved.y)
                var high = float2(moved.z, moved.w)

                while (length < left && Inside(nextX, nextY, width, height)) {
                    val tapX = Math.Address(nextX, width, wrapX)
                    val tapY = Math.Address(nextY, height, wrapY)
                    val tap = Record(tapX, tapY)

                    if (tap.x < 0f) {
                        break
                    }

                    val reached = Moved(tap, nextX, nextY, tapX, tapY, width, height)

                    low = min(low, float2(reached.x, reached.y))
                    high = max(high, float2(reached.z, reached.w))
                    length = length + 1
                    nextX = nextX + stepX
                    nextY = nextY + stepY
                }

                val settled = Settled(float4(low.x, low.y, high.x, high.y), width, height)

                for (at in 0 .. length - 1) {
                    val writtenX = Math.Address(x + (at * stepX), width, wrapX)
                    val writtenY = Math.Address(y + (at * stepY), height, wrapY)

                    target.Store(int2(writtenX, writtenY), settled)
                }
            }

            x = nextX
            y = nextY
            left = left - length
        }
    }
}
